#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133608 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133799 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74efec6b-6e5f-5761-a116-5c94e3fb4326" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System"}] :question/id #uuid "e7fb27f0-67b3-58fa-a582-acd4a4ebe045" :question/math-academy-id "e-5588" :question/problem "Find the least-squares solution to the system of linear equations given below. You're given that the columns of the corresponding coefficient matrix are linearly independent.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=12 \\\\ {x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}=-12 \\\\ {x}_{1}-{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $A \\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}\\mathbin{:}$\n![](images/cf/cf50ed91023704d55ddb9874e9c2bd081bce5ab28fb556019d038e55f32c0905.svg)\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution ${\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by $A^T\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix $A^T \\! A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $(A^T \\! A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\,\\,\\,\\,[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -12 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}-24 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 24\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-24 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 24\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133609 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133800 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d23c4292-6fdd-5cba-a2e7-0f4d770b2309" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System Given in Matrix Form"}] :question/id #uuid "9f8a0e56-d0c5-56dd-9204-c13922ec8ee4" :question/math-academy-id "e-5589" :question/problem "Given that the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, find the least-squares solution of $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$, where \n\n![](images/e3/e3892b772fb778d209832e6f5e8337186a4343ff8f550c6f8457edb9ae0311e3.svg)" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $A \\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}\\mathbin{:}$\n![](images/93/932bb863f1e3c1e59d7d0afc649768f193f8349c94c6e70ae1262ed5011ac34b.svg)\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution ${\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by $A^T\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix $A^T \\! A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $(A^T \\! A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\,\\,\\,[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}6 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133663 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133848 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5924037a-ddc3-5f50-b4b0-1bd813cef255" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Distance Between a Vector to a Subspace of Solutions of a Linear System"}] :question/id #uuid "af2b3f92-fed5-5a50-b662-eaadd9b7adbc" :question/math-academy-id "e-7429" :question/problem "Consider the system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$, where the matrix $A$ and the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ are given below. Given that the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, find the minimum value of $\\Vert A\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{b} \\Vert.$\n![](images/3e/3e5e0da0f8e23d412833c26dc9967e443fdccb98d347eaa0481f94294093341b.svg)" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $A \\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}22 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}} \\\\ Ax & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution ${\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by $A^T\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix $A^T \\! A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $(A^T \\! A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\,\\,\\,\\,[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}22 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}20 \\\\ 20\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}20 \\\\ 20\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}20 \\\\ 20\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe minimum value of $\\Vert A\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{b} \\Vert$ is attained when $\\mathbf{x}={\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}},$ which is the least-squares solution of the system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nIn this case, $A{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}$ represents the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{b}$ onto the column space of $A,$ and $\\Vert A{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}} - \\mathbf{b} \\Vert$ is the distance between the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ and the column space.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\overset{^}{x}-b & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}22 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-12 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\Vert A{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}-\\mathbf{b}\\Vert =\\sqrt{(-12)^2+0^2+(-12)^2+0^2}=12\\sqrt{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365066 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133848 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5924037a-ddc3-5f50-b4b0-1bd813cef255" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Distance Between a Vector to a Subspace of Solutions of a Linear System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49a7bd03-adf3-5a48-adb8-d76ed491a021" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365068} {:answer/id #uuid "85fe827a-334d-563d-b3ec-f6aa090b4374" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365069} {:answer/id #uuid "e16238df-b586-5a3a-b10f-36fe66a14e8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365070} {:answer/id #uuid "5a641c91-fc27-54cb-8452-020ac9399775" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365071} {:answer/id #uuid "76531132-2206-5f04-81b7-c44855988b0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365072}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "49a7bd03-adf3-5a48-adb8-d76ed491a021" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365068} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd7d2fbd-4032-5e0e-9ec2-d6247e90383e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365067}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "653b302d-1e3a-540d-aafd-23e5eba2e8bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-140952" :question/problem "Consider the system $Ax=b$, where the matrix $A$ and the vector $b$ are given below. Given that the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, find the minimum value of $‖Ax-b‖.$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}} \\\\ Ax & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}6 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 9\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{15}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe minimum value of $‖Ax-b‖$ is attained when $x=\\overset{^}{x},$ which is the least-squares solution of the system $Ax=b.$ In this case, $A\\overset{^}{x}$ represents the orthogonal projection of $b$ onto the column space of $A,$ and $‖A\\overset{^}{x}-b‖$ is the distance between the vector $b$ and the column space. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\overset{^}{x}-b & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n‖A\\overset{^}{x}-b‖=\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+(-2)^{2}}=\\sqrt{21}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326888 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133800 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d23c4292-6fdd-5cba-a2e7-0f4d770b2309" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System Given in Matrix Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1274a766-08a6-5358-9d4c-9485b12b5bfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324138} {:answer/id #uuid "48e8f4b0-d676-54a4-9bbc-31abc7596526" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{15} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324563} {:answer/id #uuid "62a3e074-a0b0-51f5-8b2d-f77ed2028e29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{15}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324791} {:answer/id #uuid "63cf970a-089a-56af-a32d-3a6bd5814f8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324799} {:answer/id #uuid "7709a30c-3f4d-576e-960b-746236bbaf59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324961}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63cf970a-089a-56af-a32d-3a6bd5814f8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324799} :answer-field/id #uuid "f89337bc-53b6-5eaa-9c9e-c82ac5b169cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326663}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c5b28021-7745-5804-91ca-f5acfea13aef" :question/math-academy-id "q-40887" :question/problem "Given that the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, find the least-squares solution of $Ax=b$, where \n\n$\\,$ $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 15\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{45}[\\begin{aligned}15 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{45}[\\begin{aligned}15 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366139 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133799 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74efec6b-6e5f-5761-a116-5c94e3fb4326" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f89186f2-808a-5375-8e88-e550363fce40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{14}{5}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366141} {:answer/id #uuid "f42a187f-745b-573a-b259-553eca42c748" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{7} \\\\ \\frac{15}{7}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366142} {:answer/id #uuid "b22c97de-bc92-57d0-9907-f0df3f1e361a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{8}{7} \\\\ -\\frac{17}{7}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366143} {:answer/id #uuid "7b1c84c5-e828-5502-92d3-09a5093a24f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{7} \\\\ -\\frac{13}{7}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366144} {:answer/id #uuid "6e6691ca-1b91-54eb-9b4a-1d71869a931e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ \\frac{11}{10}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366145}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b22c97de-bc92-57d0-9907-f0df3f1e361a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{8}{7} \\\\ -\\frac{17}{7}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366143} :answer-field/id #uuid "a32a3d55-36be-5d78-b503-164927b0eb6a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366140}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fabfecf1-3acc-5118-b86a-e7a7a1f74b45" :question/math-academy-id "q-41778" :question/problem "Find the least-squares solution to the system of linear equations given below. You're given that the columns of the corresponding coefficient matrix are linearly independent.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-4 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}6 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{14}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{8}{7} \\\\ -\\frac{17}{7}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392422 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133799 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74efec6b-6e5f-5761-a116-5c94e3fb4326" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "67f320fe-396a-55a6-bb27-1a3f5b770ee1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392424} {:answer/id #uuid "c8635233-fd1e-577d-beff-9196e75b3ecc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392425} {:answer/id #uuid "2531cfd4-e761-5c2a-93b5-c5c0d3e904c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{14} \\\\ \\frac{1}{7}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392426} {:answer/id #uuid "5dbe0276-3eb6-514f-98ee-e9513b2b1ec4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{7} \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392427} {:answer/id #uuid "d815ad2e-7dfc-57f4-a39d-ea49c417bed8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ \\frac{4}{7}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392428}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c8635233-fd1e-577d-beff-9196e75b3ecc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392425} :answer-field/id #uuid "d92ae0bd-aff1-5837-a319-be90774d548e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392423}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e0174f23-e3c1-53ee-8e26-06192fa2ac03" :question/math-academy-id "q-41779" :question/problem "Find the least-squares solution to the system of linear equations given below. You're given that the columns of the corresponding coefficient matrix are linearly independent.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}=-2 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=6 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}14 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 21\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}14 \\\\ -21\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{42}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}14 \\\\ -21\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326909 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133799 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74efec6b-6e5f-5761-a116-5c94e3fb4326" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "383e5550-6206-5528-ade3-394d4573fe0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ \\frac{11}{25}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324428} {:answer/id #uuid "b227f854-9e16-5626-bce0-ada038d08da2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325494} {:answer/id #uuid "b3a49e9d-022f-5b7d-94c6-abbb47e7bec8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{25} \\\\ \\frac{1}{75}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325514} {:answer/id #uuid "c031b973-4665-5e46-9b49-3b095d1d501d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ \\frac{19}{25}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325625} {:answer/id #uuid "de40eb95-e222-557c-aadb-9cdef97d6b8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325910}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b227f854-9e16-5626-bce0-ada038d08da2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325494} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e1a0373-21c1-5089-8385-c28dcb149d26" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326346}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b2442a57-a3cd-5f70-ab1c-b200915b57c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-41780" :question/problem "Find the least-squares solution to the system of linear equations given below. You're given that the columns of the corresponding coefficient matrix are linearly independent.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}=-7 \\\\ -{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=-4 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=12\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]}}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -2 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -2 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}14 & -16 \\\\ -16 & 29\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}26 \\\\ -19\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{150}[\\begin{aligned}29 & 16 \\\\ 16 & 14\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}26 \\\\ -19\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366497 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133848 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5924037a-ddc3-5f50-b4b0-1bd813cef255" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Distance Between a Vector to a Subspace of Solutions of a Linear System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "84388de8-73a2-569b-ab0e-277620cde274" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366499} {:answer/id #uuid "382fb806-1978-579d-89ed-efd0a6e81ee1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366500} {:answer/id #uuid "0472a83d-65e5-5013-b866-8c431be4a9a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366501} {:answer/id #uuid "34dc0ee8-1ffc-5642-9583-1a5c8c9e0ce6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366502} {:answer/id #uuid "11152628-3c35-5a9f-9807-8452a3f695de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366503}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "11152628-3c35-5a9f-9807-8452a3f695de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366503} :answer-field/id #uuid "43418a64-7ce6-5fff-bf83-32f55a513389" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366498}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e6023681-5e35-5922-859f-00465fe3c2bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-85281" :question/problem "Consider the system $Ax=b$, where the matrix $A$ and the vector $b$ are given below. Given that the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, find the minimum value of $‖Ax-b‖.$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}} \\\\ Ax & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe minimum value of $‖Ax-b‖$ is attained when $x=\\overset{^}{x},$ which is the least-squares solution of the system $Ax=b.$ In this case, $A\\overset{^}{x}$ represents the orthogonal projection of $b$ onto the column space of $A,$ and $‖A\\overset{^}{x}-b‖$ is the distance between the vector $b$ and the column space. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\overset{^}{x}-b & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n‖A\\overset{^}{x}-b‖=\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{3}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+(-1)^{2}}=3\\sqrt{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394710 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133800 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d23c4292-6fdd-5cba-a2e7-0f4d770b2309" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System Given in Matrix Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "baa3ba28-76ba-5cdf-8ee0-dd2cae767c11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394712} {:answer/id #uuid "fc176378-6142-5511-99d4-efd81e325265" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394713} {:answer/id #uuid "734a16e6-4f2d-5aff-a58a-27039fba253b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394714} {:answer/id #uuid "93ea5882-013b-54a0-8397-c4f9dda4c879" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394715} {:answer/id #uuid "763ad4cd-05a7-54d8-83ae-44143e161aeb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394716}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "763ad4cd-05a7-54d8-83ae-44143e161aeb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394716} :answer-field/id #uuid "63b3271f-d2b5-5926-a3b0-440fe31dff0b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394711}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c761d373-1b10-5d00-ac6a-15cc406ca2c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-85895" :question/problem "Given that the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, find the least-squares solution of $Ax=b$, where \n\n$\\,$ $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394717 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133799 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74efec6b-6e5f-5761-a116-5c94e3fb4326" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "022fd928-0862-5733-ab58-6ac021653bf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394719} {:answer/id #uuid "b2b652e4-fd78-5105-b9b1-bb833b9d44c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394720} {:answer/id #uuid "5a900c06-a1b0-545a-a21b-2f25f3f3df23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394721} {:answer/id #uuid "692f0623-c254-5654-8935-aa6f78aec536" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394722} {:answer/id #uuid "a5146e89-5254-5149-9ac3-ddec866654a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394723}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "022fd928-0862-5733-ab58-6ac021653bf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394719} :answer-field/id #uuid "0517efea-8701-5fb5-afbc-671977da3a47" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394718}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9e19f906-5855-5ce0-b304-b58ec502b93a" :question/math-academy-id "q-85903" :question/problem "Find the least-squares solution to the system of linear equations given below. You're given that the columns of the corresponding coefficient matrix are linearly independent.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=9 \\\\ {x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354440 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133848 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5924037a-ddc3-5f50-b4b0-1bd813cef255" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Distance Between a Vector to a Subspace of Solutions of a Linear System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15daec31-7862-5263-80db-2090dc8fbbd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351758} {:answer/id #uuid "40225e20-962a-5ae3-8315-535b48344c65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352161} {:answer/id #uuid "46ff8bf7-6336-5516-8b73-cfe228faf760" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352223} {:answer/id #uuid "c842ab27-883d-524b-b73e-ecdeb8f933ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353309} {:answer/id #uuid "e98a1f81-e483-53e1-8bae-170bf4fa1ee5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353600}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c842ab27-883d-524b-b73e-ecdeb8f933ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353309} :answer-field/id #uuid "478d5745-16e4-5393-ada0-6f0f2aa4b32a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353942}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8fe94dab-ed8a-5caf-8874-c5b5c72bebb6" :question/math-academy-id "q-85909" :question/problem "Consider the system $Ax=b$, where the matrix $A$ and the vector $b$ are given below. Given that the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, find the minimum value of $‖Ax-b‖.$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}} \\\\ Ax & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe minimum value of $‖Ax-b‖$ is attained when $x=\\overset{^}{x},$ which is the least-squares solution of the system $Ax=b.$ In this case, $A\\overset{^}{x}$ represents the orthogonal projection of $b$ onto the column space of $A,$ and $‖A\\overset{^}{x}-b‖$ is the distance between the vector $b$ and the column space. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\overset{^}{x}-b & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n‖A\\overset{^}{x}-b‖=\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}=\\sqrt{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382573 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133848 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5924037a-ddc3-5f50-b4b0-1bd813cef255" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Distance Between a Vector to a Subspace of Solutions of a Linear System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "db34a3a7-82db-516e-9b5f-f74695de47f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{19}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382575} {:answer/id #uuid "231e106c-0303-5b69-9237-341c2dc82244" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382576} {:answer/id #uuid "b1264692-a197-5024-84dd-8409773b4cef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382577} {:answer/id #uuid "63c436ce-c5cd-5684-9714-4a097df7fdbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{19}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382578} {:answer/id #uuid "4dce82c7-9228-540c-b08c-9cc8383746e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382579}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db34a3a7-82db-516e-9b5f-f74695de47f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{19}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382575} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f3514e1-3487-5717-8374-c6a14d4839ec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382574}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7ed86ff9-5c1b-5f36-88cb-573447ebe9b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-85919" :question/problem "Consider the system $Ax=b$, where the matrix $A$ and the vector $b$ are shown below. Given that the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, find the minimum value of $‖Ax-b‖.$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 13 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 13 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]}} \\\\ Ax & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\,\\,[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 13 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 10\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{36}[\\begin{aligned}10 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe minimum value of $‖Ax-b‖$ is attained when $x=\\overset{^}{x},$ which is the least-squares solution of the system $Ax=b.$ In this case, $A\\overset{^}{x}$ represents the orthogonal projection of $b$ onto the column space of $A,$ and $‖A\\overset{^}{x}-b‖$ is the distance between the vector $b$ and the column space. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\overset{^}{x}-b & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 13 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -9 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n‖A\\overset{^}{x}-b‖=\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+(-5)^{2}+(-9)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-8)^{2}}=3\\sqrt{19}.\n$$"}]}
